hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2951","**
Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Ibn Jurayj.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Shihab saying: 'Isa ibn Talhah informed me, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ), while delivering a sermon on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Nahr), a man stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I did not think that such-and-such should come before such-and-such.""

Then another man stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I did not think that such-and-such should come before such-and-such.""

Regarding these three instances, the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Do it, and there is no harm.""

This is the narration of 'Isa. Ibn Ma'mar added in his narration: The Prophet (ﷺ) was not asked about anything on that day except that he said: ""Do it, and there is no harm."""
"2952","**
Abu Bakr (RA) said in a narration from Ibn Sirin, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father (Abu Bakrah RA), and from Humaid ibn Abdur-Rahman, from Abu Bakrah (RA):

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered a lengthy sermon on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm an-Nahr) in Mina.

**NARRATION CHAIN:**
Narrated to us Bundar, who said:
Narrated to us Abu 'Amir, who said:
Narrated to us Qurrah, who said:
From Muhammad ibn Sirin, who narrated:
From Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father (Abu Bakrah RA), and
From Humaid ibn Abdur-Rahman, from Abu Bakrah (RA).

---"
